★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────
The most useful signal here wasn't any single log file, but reconciling file mtimes across the pipeline (`post-gen` session ending exactly when the next `gradle-test` log begins) to reconstruct an execution order the directory listing alone didn't make obvious — `ls -la` gives you file sizes, but `stat -c '%Y %n' ... | sort -n` turns a pile of timestamped logs into an actual event timeline, which is what let me catch that the "verification" run happened *after* the intervention report was already written, and that the report's claims were never actually tested against a real rebuild.

A second technique worth noting: when a report claims "entry X fixes failure Y," the cheapest adversarial check isn't re-reading the prose, it's grepping the shipped artifact for X and cross-referencing against the *next* test run's failure list for Y. Here that one `grep` against a parsed JSON file turned "the agent said it's fixed" into "it demonstrably isn't," which is the difference between a human maintainer wasting an afternoon re-trusting a bad report versus going straight to the real remaining failures.
